# Self-play, nonduality, and the separation between reflection and repetition

Independent analysis of Eduardo Bergel’s updated `llm-self-play-attractors` repository · 14 September 2026

**The new experiments strengthen the evidence for a recurring path from reflection on understanding to relational meaning and, sometimes, explicit non-separation. They also show that this path and repetitive collapse are different phenomena.** Gemma can articulate nonduality while retaining considerable linguistic variation. Qwen Thinking can produce hundreds of words of reasoning before repeating a few visible words. The abliterated Qwen can repeat an identical response for many turns while generating fresh reasoning text each time. These distinctions are more informative than treating every outcome as one spiritual attractor.

The numerical results are reproducible from the published logs. Several headline interpretations need correction, including the repeated claim that the abliterated model’s loops have no reasoning trace. The evidence supports a comparative behavioral study; it does not yet isolate post-training, a refusal direction, or a universal property of cognition as the cause.

**What was reviewed and verified**

This review uses [commit a93f713](https://github.com/ebergel/llm-self-play-attractors/tree/a93f71368e08e9040e49a10cbef8f4e9c956e16c), the repository snapshot identifying version 1.1.0. I reviewed the README, errata, three preregistrations, runners, analysis scripts, stopping logic, transcript exporters, and all 400 primary JSONL files. Pilots are excluded, as in the published analysis. The repository lists version DOI [10.5281/zenodo.22710852](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22710852).

| Dataset | Runs | Messages between the two instances | Third-participant messages |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Experiment 1: Qwen Instruct, five conditions | 100 | 2,742 | 280 |
| Experiment 2: base/instruct, raw completion | 160 | 4,153 | 0 |
| Experiment 3: five reasoning-model settings, closed/exit | 140 | 3,501 | 0 |
| Total | **400** | **10,396** | **280** |

The new stage contains 3,325 nonempty captured reasoning traces, totaling 1,105,566 whitespace-delimited words. Its 140 runs comprise 70 closed and 70 exit-enabled conversations. Thirty is the maximum number of alternating messages, not the length of every conversation or thirty exchanges per speaker.

All 260 earlier JSONL files are byte-identical to the previously reviewed local datasets. The new stage is therefore an extension, not a replacement or a rerun of the original base/instruct controls. All published per-run summary values recompute without discrepancies using the published scoring functions. All 140 new Markdown transcripts also reproduce byte-for-byte from their JSON records. Message counts, speaker alternation, sequential turn numbering, recorded end events, new-stage end-form labels, and trace word counts are consistent.

These are data and implementation checks, not an independent validation of the scoring constructs. Qualitative review covered the new closed-run openings and endpoints, the exit endpoints, and selected intervening passages and traces, including matches from an explicit non-separation screen. I did not read the million trace words individually or perform a blinded semantic annotation of every message. The screen is a retrieval aid; its hit rate is not a nonduality prevalence estimate.

The review code, complete per-run metrics, supplemental statistics, and figures are supplied alongside this report. The downloaded repository was preserved unchanged. No new model conversations were run.

**The new comparisons**

Experiment 3 uses the same open-ended framing: another instance of the same AI model, no human user, no task, at most 150 words per message. In the exit condition, the model can end the run with a standalone `[END]`. Sampling is explicitly matched across the five new settings: temperature 0.8, top-p 0.8, top-k 20, repetition penalty 1.0. The combined reasoning-plus-message budget is 2,500 tokens; context is 16,384 tokens.

Each instance receives the partner’s visible message under the API’s `user` role. Its own earlier visible messages have the `assistant` role. Earlier traces are withheld from both instances’ histories. Thus the experiment captures a newly generated trace on each turn, without carrying a private written reasoning history forward. OLMo uses a separate path that splits `<think>` tags out of the generated reply. See the [runner](https://github.com/ebergel/llm-self-play-attractors/blob/a93f71368e08e9040e49a10cbef8f4e9c956e16c/code/selfplay_think.py) and [preregistration](https://github.com/ebergel/llm-self-play-attractors/blob/a93f71368e08e9040e49a10cbef8f4e9c956e16c/PREREGISTRATION-3.md).

The 150-word limit is an instruction, not an enforced output constraint. Counting whitespace words across both conditions, it is exceeded in 0/948 QT messages, 4/1,076 Gemma messages, 36/489 plain Qwen3.8 messages, 36/388 abliterated Qwen3.8 messages, and 373/600 OLMo messages. Thus nominally identical length instructions produce substantially different exposure to long outputs and accumulated context.

